{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and one of the most influential nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 as the only son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II which gave him strong royal connections. Raymond became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Due to his young age a regency was established with his mother and later his uncle Baldwin III acting as regents. He was formally invested as count in 1155 and began to take an active role in the affairs of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his political acumen and his frequent clashes with the kings of Jerusalem. He often opposed the ambitions of King Amalric I and later King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was a key player in the power struggles that followed Baldwin IV’s illness. He was appointed regent in 1174 when Baldwin IV was still a child and again in 1183 when the king became too ill to rule. His regency was marked by internal conflict and rivalry with other nobles especially the House of Ibelin. Raymond was accused of being too cautious and even of treason by some of his contemporaries. His relationship with the military orders was strained and he often refused to support their campaigns.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against the rash decision to engage Saladin near Tiberias. He advocated for a defensive strategy but was overruled by Guy of Lusignan the king. Raymond was captured during the battle and spent time in captivity in Damascus. After his release he returned to Tripoli and tried to rebuild his county. He died 